<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; border: 2px solid #1a5276;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # How Are You? – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I'm Happy.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Are You?</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Engage in interactive labeling activities.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can trace letters and words that describe emotions, with teacher guidance (e.g., 'sad' or 'happy').<br>
            Can engage in interactive labeling activities by matching words to pictures of emotions (e.g., labeling 'happy' under a smiling face).<br>
            Can draw and label facial expressions (e.g., 'sad' or 'angry').
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by playing a "How Are You?" song (e.g., "If You're Happy and You Know It"). Students sing along and act out emotions (happy, sad, angry, tired). The teacher shows flashcards of different facial expressions (happy, sad, angry, sleepy, excited) and asks, "How are you?". Students respond chorally or individually with "I'm happy!" or "I'm sad!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a happy face and models the word 'happy', pointing to the word written on the card. "This is 'happy'." The teacher traces the word 'happy' in the air and says, "H-A-P-P-Y, happy." The teacher emphasizes the /h/ sound in 'happy' and the /e/ sound in 'excited' when reviewing words. For phonemic awareness, the teacher highlights the letter 'H' in 'happy'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a happy face flashcard) "Is this face sad or happy?" (Expected answer: "Happy!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing the written word 'sad') "Is this 'happy' or 'sad'?" (Expected answer: "Sad!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large picture cards showing different emotions (happy, sad, angry, tired, excited) with the corresponding word printed clearly below each picture. Worksheets with dotted lines for tracing letters and words, and sections for drawing and labeling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher presents large picture cards one by one. For each card (e.g., a smiling face), the teacher says, "This is a happy face. The word is 'happy'. I'm happy." The teacher points to the word 'happy' and explicitly traces it with a finger. This process is repeated for 'sad', 'angry', 'tired', and 'excited'. The teacher also reinforces simple sentences like 'I'm happy.' and 'I'm tired.' by asking, 'How are you?' and having students respond based on the picture. The letter 'E' in 'excited' and 'H' in 'happy' are briefly highlighted during this stage for phonemic awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can trace and copy simple words related to emotions and label basic facial expressions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a picture of a sad face and asks, "Point to 'sad'." Students point to the word 'sad' from a selection of word cards.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Which word says 'happy'?" and students point to the correct word written on a flashcard.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Tracing letters and words; identifying and writing words for emotions (happy, sad, angry, tired, excited). Grammar: Simple sentences 'I'm + adjective'. Vocabulary: happy, sad, tired, angry, excited. Phonemic Awareness: Recognizing letters (E, H).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Tracing):</b> Students receive a worksheet with large dotted-line versions of the words 'happy', 'sad', 'tired', 'angry', 'excited'. The teacher demonstrates how to trace each letter carefully. Students use pencils or crayons to trace the words. The teacher circulates, providing individual guidance and encouraging correct letter formation, highlighting 'H' in 'happy' and 'E' in 'excited'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Matching & Tracing):</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of different emotion faces (happy, sad, angry) and a word bank at the bottom with the words 'happy', 'sad', 'angry' (written clearly, not dotted). Students are instructed to draw a line from each picture to the correct word in the word bank. After matching, they will copy the correct word from the word bank onto a line provided under each picture, engaging in interactive labeling activities.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs. They show their completed matching and tracing worksheets to their friend. Each student points to a picture and says the emotion, "This is happy," or "This is sad." The partner verifies if the word is correctly matched and copied. The teacher guides them by saying, "Show your friend your 'happy' face and say 'I'm happy!'"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-20 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Drawing and Labeling Activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a blank sheet of paper and are asked to "Draw your friend. How is your friend feeling today?" or "Draw a face showing how you feel." They choose one emotion (e.g., happy, sad, angry). After drawing the facial expression, they attempt to write the corresponding word (e.g., 'happy', 'sad', 'angry') below their drawing. The teacher provides a visual word bank on the board with 'happy', 'sad', 'angry' written clearly for students to copy from. The focus is on their attempt to draw and label, allowing for varied spelling attempts at this level. The teacher circulates, offering support and encouragement, asking "How does your friend feel?" and guiding them to write the correct word from the word bank. This connects directly to the theme "How Are You?" and scenario "I'm Happy." as students express and label feelings.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and collection of student worksheets/drawings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their drawing and labeling activity from the Production stage and their matching/tracing worksheet. The teacher reviews these for evidence of tracing ability, correct matching, and attempts at independent labeling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can trace letters and words (e.g., 'happy', 'sad').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can match words to pictures of emotions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can attempt to draw and label a facial expression.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort and participation in tracing and labeling activities.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher shows pictures of 'happy' and 'sad' faces. Students receive a small paper with the words 'happy' and 'sad' written. They circle the word that describes how they feel right now. The teacher asks, "Did you trace nicely today?" (Thumbs up/down). "Did you try to write the words?" (Thumbs up/down).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word did you like to write the most today?" (Students can point to their word or say it).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What makes you feel happy?" (Simple one-word answers or gestures).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in supporting students' writing of emotion words? The tracing activities were engaging for most students. What adjustments are needed for next time? Some students struggled with letter formation for 'g' in 'angry' and 'x' in 'excited' when copying – might need more individual hand-over-hand guidance or simpler words next time. Scaffolds for independent writing (e.g., providing a word bank) were effective for some, but others needed more direct copying. I should prepare larger, clearer word banks for copying.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will build on identifying and expressing feelings by encouraging students to share their drawings and explain their feelings, mediating simple information and preparing for a collaborative project about emotions and helping friends.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>